Let $\mathrm{Cyc}_{
e k}$ be the species of cycles of length not $k$. Then permutations with no $k$-cycle form $\mathrm{Set}\circ\mathrm{Cyc}_{
e k}$. Since $\mathrm{Cyc}=\mathrm{Cyc}_{
e k}+\mathrm{Cyc}_k$ and $\mathrm{Per}=\mathrm{Set}\circ\mathrm{Cyc}$ has EGF $ rac1{1-z}=e^{\mathrm{Cyc}(z)}$, we get $\mathrm{Cyc}(z)=\ln rac1{1-z}$ and $\mathrm{Cyc}_{
e k}(z)=\ln rac1{1-z}- rac{z^k}k$ (using the EGF of $\mathrm{Cyc}_k$, which is $ rac{z^k}{k}$). Therefore (Theorem 3)
$$\hat P_k(z)=\exp\left(\ln	frac1{1-z}-	frac{z^k}kight)= rac{e^{-z^k/k}}{1-z}.$$

**Telescoping.** $(1-z)\hat P_k(z)=\sum_{n}\hat P_k(n) rac{z^n}{n!}-\sum_n\hat P_k(n) rac{z^{n+1}}{n!}$. Its $N$-th partial sum at $z=1$ is $\sum_{n=0}^N rac{\hat P_k(n)}{n!}-\sum_{n=0}^{N-1} rac{\hat P_k(n)}{n!}= rac{\hat P_k(N)}{N!}=P_k(N)$. Therefore $P_k(N)$ equals the $N$-th partial sum of $(1-z)\hat P_k(z)$ at $z=1$, whose limit (the value of the series, assuming limits commute, as in the official solution) is $\left.e^{-z^k/k}ight|_{z=1}=e^{-1/k}$. ∎
